            <ArticleTitle>[Surgical treatment of giant coronary artery aneurysm].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1535-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To discuss the clinical features and surgical treatments of giant coronary artery aneurysm (CAA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">From July 1996 to October 2004, 6 giant CAA patients were underwent surgery at Fuwai hospital. Three cases were underwent CAA resection, 2 concomitant coronary bypass, 3 reconstruction. The giant CAA was often combined with other cardiac diseases. Four cases underwent additional procedures of fistula closure, 3 aortic valve replacements, 2 aortoplasty and 1 thrombus cleaning at the same time.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All patients recovered uneventfully. The mean of cardiopulmonary bypass time was (144 +/- 26) min (range 67 to 207 min). Aortic cross clamping time was (104 +/- 21) min (range 56 to 172 min). Patients follow-up time occurred from 8 to 87 months (mean of 48 months). All patients were free of symptoms during follow-up. None of the patients died during the follow-up period and none of the CAA recurred.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The giant CAA is a serious cardiovascular disease, early diagnosis and surgical treatment are mandatory.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
